Arsenal entered the competition in the third round, receiving a bye as a [[Premier League]] club. Their opening match was a 2–0 home win against Oxford United on 4 January 2003.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2607165.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=4 January 2003 |access-date=12 March 2012 |title=Arsenal end Oxford dream}}</ref> Striker [[Dennis Bergkamp]] scored his 100th goal for the club and an own goal by defender [[Scott McNiven]] ensured progression to the next round.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/jan/06/match.sport3 |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |location=London |date=6 January 2003 |access-date=12 March 2012 |first=Simon |last=Burnton  |title=Bergkamp worthy of ton of respect}}</ref> Arsenal faced non-league side [[Farnborough F.C.|Farnborough Town]]; the match switched from Farnborough's ground at [[Cherrywood Road]] to [[Arsenal Stadium|Highbury]] due to concerns over safety.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/jan/09/sport.media |work=[[The Guardian]]|date=9 January 2003 |access-date=12 March 2012 |last=Bradley |first=Mark |title=Farnborough to switch cup tie}}</ref> Farnborough began the match as the home team and conceded the first goal, scored by Arsenal defender [[Sol Campbell]] in the 19th minute. They went down to ten men after [[Christian Lee (footballer)|Christian Lee]] was sent off for a professional foul in the 28th minute. [[Francis Jeffers]] scored twice before [[Rocky Baptiste]] added a consolation, beating [[Pascal Cygan]] for pace and despite having his first shot saved by goalkeeper [[Stuart Taylor (footballer born 1980)|Stuart Taylor]], he managed to lift the ball over him and into the net. [[Lauren (Cameroonian footballer)|Lauren]] and Bergkamp each scored in the final 15 minutes to give Arsenal a 5–1 victory.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2671825.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=25 January 2003 |access-date=12 March 2012 |title=Arsenal cruise through}}</ref>

Arsenal's fifth round match was away to league rivals [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] at [[Old Trafford]] on 16 February 2003. After [[Ryan Giggs]] missed the chance to score past an open goal, midfielder [[Edu (footballer, born 1978)|Edu]] gave Arsenal the lead through a free kick which took a deflection off [[David Beckham]]'s shoulder. Striker [[Sylvain Wiltord]] scored the second goal of the match in the 52nd minute, running onto a pass from Edu and side-footing the ball past goalkeeper [[Fabien Barthez]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2740035.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=16 February 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=Arsenal cruise past Man Utd}}</ref> Arsenal captain [[Patrick Vieira]] said of the performance: "We knew when we lost here in the league that we had lost the battle in midfield. We had to put that right, and we did."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/feb/16/match.sport1|newspaper=[[The Observer]] |location=London |date=16 February 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |last=Wilson |first=Paul |title=Arsenal triumph as Giggs goes missing}}</ref> In the sixth round, Arsenal was drawn at home to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in a repeat of the [[2002 FA Cup final|previous season's final]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2769829.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=27 February 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=London giants collide}}</ref> Chelsea defender [[John Terry]] put Chelsea ahead with a [[Glossary of association football terms#H|header]] from a [[Set piece (football)|set piece]] before Arsenal responded through Jeffers and [[Thierry Henry]]. [[Frank Lampard]] scored a late equaliser for the visiting team meaning the match was [[Replay (sports)|replayed]] at [[Stamford Bridge (football)|Stamford Bridge]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/2805009.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=8 March 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=Chelsea hold Arsenal}}</ref> An own goal by Terry and a strike by Wiltord in the space of seven minutes during the replay gave Arsenal an early lead against Chelsea. Despite going down to ten men after Cygan was sent off and Terry scoring from a header, the away team scored a third goal through Lauren to ensure progression into the semi-finals.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2873509.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=25 March 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=Arsenal brush aside Chelsea}}</ref> In the semi-final against [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]] on 13 April 2003 at Old Trafford, [[Freddie Ljungberg]] scored the winning goal to help Arsenal reach their third successive FA Cup final appearance.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/2917575.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=13 April 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=Arsenal sink brave Blades}}</ref> The match was best remembered for [[David Seaman]]'s late save, which prevented Sheffield United from equalising.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/apr/14/match.sport9 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=14 April 2003 |access-date=12 March 2012 |last=McCarra |first=Kevin |title=Super Seaman defies time and gravity to end Blades odyssey}}</ref>

