Like Arsenal, as a Premier League club, Southampton received a bye into the third round. Their opening match was a 4–0 win against fellow league club [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]]. A goal by defender [[Michael Svensson]] and three from [[Jo Tessem]], [[Anders Svensson (footballer, born 1976)|Anders Svensson]] and [[James Beattie (footballer)|James Beattie]] in the second half was the second straight victory against Tottenham, having beaten them on New Year's Day in the league.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2607231.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=4 January 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=Saints thrash Spurs}}</ref> In the fourth round, Southampton was drawn at home to [[Football League First Division|First Division]] club [[Millwall F.C.|Millwall]] on 25 January 2003. The visitors took the lead through striker [[Steve Claridge]] but were denied victory. 90 seconds from the end of the match as Southampton striker [[Kevin Davies]] scored from a rebounded shot.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2671837.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=25 January 2003 |access-date=13 March 2012 |title=Last-gasp Saints deny Lions}}</ref> In the replay, midfielder [[Matthew Oakley]] scored twice for Southampton (one in both halves) either side of a [[Steven Reid]] equaliser for Millwall.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2708849.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=13 February 2003 |access-date=17 March 2012 |title=Saints tame Lions}}</ref>

Southampton's fifth round match was against [[Norwich City F.C.|Norwich City]] at home on 5 February 2003. Two goals in the space of three minutes, scored by Anders Svensson and Jo Tessem was enough to take the team into the quarter-finals.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2740041.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=5 February 2003 |access-date=17 March 2012 |title=Saints see off Norwich}}</ref> Southampton defender [[Claus Lundekvam]] was pleased with the win and said following the match: "When you get to this stage in the competition you have to believe you can win it."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2768269.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=16 February 2003 |access-date=17 March 2012 |title=Lundekvam eyes FA Cup glory}}</ref> The club then faced [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]] at home in the following round. Former Wolves player [[Chris Marsden]] gave Southampton the lead right in the 56th minute and with nine minutes remaining of normal time, the team added a second goal when Jo Tessem's shot took a deflection off [[Paul Butler (footballer born 1972)|Paul Butler]]'s legs to go inside the goal net.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/mar/10/match.sport8 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=10 March 2003 |access-date=17 March 2012 |last=Brodkin|first=Jon |title=Super Wolves softened up by old boy Marsden}}</ref> The victory meant Southampton reached the semi-finals of the FA Cup for the first time in 17 years.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/2805037.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=9 March 2003 |access-date=17 March 2012 |title=Saints tame Wolves}}</ref> At [[Villa Park]], Southampton played First Division team [[Watford F.C.|Watford]] on 13 April 2003. Brett Ormerod opened the scoring two minutes before half time and set up the second goal which saw the ball being taken over the line by Watford defender [[Paul Robinson (footballer born December 1978)|Paul Robinson]]. Despite [[Marcus Gayle]] scoring a late header to half the scoreline Southampton won the match.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2399604/Ormerod-destroys-Watford-dream.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |first=Christopher |last=Davies |location=London |date=14 April 2003 |access-date=18 March 2012 |title=Ormerod destroys Watford dream}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
[[File:Bobby_Robson_Cropped.jpg|thumb|180px|Sir [[Bobby Robson]] was the chief guest for the final, and presented the trophy to the winners]]
Arsenal was appearing in the final of the FA Cup for the sixteenth time. They had won the cup eight times previously (in [[1930 FA Cup final|1930]], [[1936 FA Cup final|1936]], [[1950 FA Cup final|1950]], [[1971 FA Cup final|1971]], [[1979 FA Cup final|1979]], [[1993 FA Cup final|1993]], [[1998 FA Cup final|1998]] and [[2002 FA Cup final|2002]]) and had been beaten in the final seven times, the most recent in [[2001 FA Cup final|2001]]. By comparison, Southampton made their fourth appearance in a FA Cup final. Their previous best was winning the cup in [[1976 FA Cup final|1976]], by beating opponents Manchester United.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2404192/Saints-marching-on-the-giants.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |first=Paul |last=Hayward |location=London |date=17 May 2003 |access-date=18 March 2012 |title=Saints marching on the giants}}</ref>

Both clubs received an allocation of approximately 25,000 tickets, with the remaining 25,000 being sent out to other clubs.<ref name="ticks">{{cite news |title=Touts selling tickets at 10 times value |page=7 |newspaper=South Wales Echo |location=Cardiff |date=7 May 2003}}</ref> 17,500 of those tickets were available to Southampton season ticket holders.<ref name="tisk"/> Seat prices for the final exceeded £80, with the cheapest tickets available at £25.<ref name="tisk"/> Southampton was given the South Stand, which was the larger end of the stadium, whereas Arsenal was situated at the opposite end.<ref name="tisk">{{cite news |title=25,000 tickets for Saints' Cardiff army |first=Adam |last=Leitch |newspaper=Daily Echo |location=Southampton |date=26 April 2003}}</ref> Although Southampton supporters were disappointed at the allocation share, chairman [[Rupert Lowe]] refused to criticise the FA's decision, by saying: "The reality is that too many people want to go and there are never enough tickets."<ref>{{cite news |title=Lowe blasts ticket fiasco |first=Harry |last=Pratt |page=71 |newspaper=Daily Star |location=London |date=7 May 2003}}</ref> In the lead up to the final, the ''[[South Wales Echo]]'' reported that many tickets were being sold on the black market, for "20 times" the face-value price.<ref name="ticks"/>

Nine days before the final, the two clubs faced each other in a league match at [[Arsenal Stadium|Highbury]] (Arsenal's former stadium). With Arsenal unable to retain the title, having lost to [[Leeds United A.F.C.|Leeds United]], manager [[Arsène Wenger]] rested several players, as did Southampton manager [[Gordon Strachan]], whose team started without six of their first-choice eleven.<ref name="highb">{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/may/08/match.sport1 |newspaper=The Guardian |first=Jon |last=Brodkin |location=London |date=8 May 2003 |access-date=18 March 2012 |title=Arsenal star in Cup dress rehearsal}}</ref> Winger [[Jermaine Pennant]] on his league debut scored a hat-trick, as did Pires, in a 6–1 win.<ref name="highb"/> Strachan believed the result had little bearing on their chances of winning the cup, noting: "There is little pressure on Southampton to lift the trophy. We were not expected to reach the final and have already clinched a place in the UEFA Cup."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/may/11/sport.facup |newspaper=The Guardian |first=Gordan |last=Strachan |location=London |date=11 May 2003 |access-date=18 March 2012 |title=Price of victory may be lack of spectacle}}</ref>

The traditional Cup Final hymn, "[[Abide with Me]]" was sung by [[Tony Henry (singer)|Tony Henry]], an opera singer from South London.<ref>{{cite news |title=Opera singer to kick off the FA Cup Final |page=26 |newspaper=South Wales Echo |location=Cardiff |date=15 May 2003}}</ref> [[Bobby Robson|Sir Bobby Robson]] was invited as the FA's chief guest for the final and performed several duties ordinarily reserved for royalty, such as presenting the trophy to the winning captain. Heavy rain on Friday night and forecasted showers in Cardiff meant the final would be the first to be played indoors; the stadium closed its retractable roof and floodlights were used to light up the ground.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup final: Day roof fell in on the Saints |page=6 |newspaper=The People |first=Frank |last=Wiechula |location=London |date=18 May 2003}}</ref>

==Match==
===Team selection===
Vieira was ruled out of the match because of a knee injury during the FA Cup semi-final match against [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]],<ref>{{Cite web|last=agencies|first=Staff and|date=2003-04-14|title=Vieira fit for United showdown|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/apr/14/newsstory.sport3|access-date=2021-04-12|website=The Guardian|language=en}}</ref> so Wenger named [[David Seaman]] as Arsenal's captain for the match. In a match widely anticipated as his final for the club.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/may/16/newsstory.sport1 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=16 May 2003 |access-date=12 March 2012 |first=Jon |last=Brodkin |title=Able Seaman is captain for final}}</ref> With Campbell suspended and Cygan absent due to a thigh strain,<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger left with defence worries |first=Steve |last=Stammers |page=63 |newspaper=Evening Standard |location=London |date=13 May 2003}}</ref> Wenger picked [[Oleh Luzhnyi]] to pair up with [[Martin Keown]], who was rested the previous Sunday away against [[Sunderland A.F.C.|Sunderland]]. For Southampton, the major absentee was their striker [[Marian Pahars]], who underwent a third operation to overcome a troubling knee injury.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup countdown: Arsenal v Southampton |first=Lee |last=West |page=68 |newspaper=Daily Mirror |location=London |date=16 May 2003}}</ref> Defender [[Chris Baird]] made his second competitive start for the club and [[Chris Marsden]] captained Southampton, given [[Jason Dodd]]'s absence with an injury. Although both teams set up in a [[Formation (association football)#4.E2.80.934.E2.80.932|4–4–2 formation]], Bergkamp was positioned as a [[Forward (association football)#Second striker|deep-lying forward]] behind Henry.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal counter friendly fire |first=Henry |last=Winter |page=B2 |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=19 May 2003}}</ref>

===Report===
[[File:Robert_Pires1.JPG|thumb|left|190px|[[Robert Pires]] scored the only goal of the final]]

Arsenal created their first chance inside 24 seconds of the match, when Ljungberg put Henry clear down the right-hand side.<ref name=repo>{{cite news|title=Arsenal leads Southampton 1–0 at half time of FA Cup final |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-73980144.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140611075037/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-73980144.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=11 June 2014 |work=AP Worldstream |publisher=Associated Press |date=17 May 2003 |access-date=1 March 2013 |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |location=Cardiff}} </ref> The striker used his pace to get the better of Lundekvam, only to have his shot blocked by Southampton goalkeeper [[Antti Niemi (footballer)|Antti Niemi]].<ref name=repo/> Bergkamp's goal effort in the eighth minute was cleared off the line by full back [[Chris Baird]], after Niemi fumbled Henry's initial shot.<ref name=repo/> Southampton fashioned their first opportunity in the 15th minute through a high cross; in spite of unsettling the Arsenal defence, the unmarked Svensson volleyed over the bar.<ref name=repo/> Baird moments after won the ball in midfield and curled a shot that left Seaman "scrambling across his goal to save". Seven minutes before the break, Arsenal went into the lead. Henry, receiving the ball from Parlour, slipped it into Bergkamp down the right. He in turn fed the ball to Ljungberg, whose shot was blocked. The ball rebounded in the direction of Pires, who took one touch to set himself and another to fire into the goal at the near post, despite Niemi getting a hand to the ball.<ref name=repo/> Arsenal missed further chances to extend their lead when a cross from the right by Henry was shot over the bar by Pires and from the same area, Bergkamp's "cross-cum-shot" was missed by Ljungberg.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/may/18/match.facup |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |date=18 May 2003 |access-date=1 March 2013 |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Pires aim is true for muted Gunners}}</ref>

After the break, Southampton applied pressure and a poor clearance by Seaman invited a chance for [[Paul Telfer (footballer)|Paul Telfer]] to shoot the ball from "35 yards out"; his pass found Ormerod, but was eventually intercepted by Luzhnyi.<ref name=das>{{Cite news |url=http://www.gazettelive.co.uk/north-east-sport/football/latest-football-news/2003/05/17/arsenal-1-southampton-0-84229-12973208/ |newspaper=Evening Gazette |location=Middlesbrough |date=17 May 2003 |access-date=1 March 2013 |title=Arsenal 1, Southampton 0 }}</ref> Minutes after, Beattie failed to take advantage from Oakley's cross, as the ball drifted wide.<ref name=das/> Arsenal regained possession and in the 52nd minute went close to doubling their lead.<ref name=repod>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2003/may/17/minutebyminute.sport |work=The Guardian|date=17 May 2003 |access-date=1 March 2013 |first=John |last=MacLeary |title=Arsenal v Southampton: minute-by-minute}}</ref> In Southampton's penalty box, Bergkamp turned and beat Ormerod before curling a shot which Niemi palmed off; it fell to the feet of Ljungberg, who shot the ball into the side-netting.<ref name=repod/> Telfer misguided his header from a Southampton corner, before Niemi denied Henry again. In the 65th minute, Niemi injured himself, in an attempt to clear the ball and was replaced by substitute [[Paul Jones (footballer, born 1967)|Paul Jones]].<ref name=repod/> Both clubs made substitutions in the final third of the game, with Wiltord coming on for Bergkamp and Tessem replacing Svensson.<ref name=repod/> Ormerod's goal-bound effort was saved by Seaman with 10 minutes remaining of the match. In the fourth minute of injury time, Southampton earned themselves a corner.<ref name=repod/> Beattie's on-target header was cleared off the line by [[Ashley Cole]] and out for another corner, which Pires kicked out in the final action of the game.<ref name=repod/>

