==Post-match==
In retaining the cup, Arsenal became the first team to do so since Tottenham Hotspur in 1982. Wenger commented after the game that his team "got the trophy we wanted" while Strachan was in admiration of Southampton's performance: "I'm very proud of the way they competed. I couldn't have asked for any more."<ref name=Post>{{cite news|title=Keown hails 'best ever' win|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/3036631.stm|access-date=16 March 2012|publisher=BBC Sport |date=17 May 2003}}</ref> Keown said the FA Cup win was "the best ever" and Seaman felt the disappointment of losing out to Manchester United in the league spurred the team on.<ref name=Post /> Football pundits [[Alan Hansen]], [[Peter Schmeichel]] and [[Mark Hughes]] unanimously agreed that Arsenal deserved to win the match.<ref>{{cite news|title=Arsenal were worthy winners|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/3036729.stm|access-date=16 March 2012|publisher=BBC Sport  |date=17 May 2003}}</ref>

Arsenal's victory set up a [[FA Community Shield|Community Shield]] match against Manchester United, the winners of the 2002–03 Premier League. The FA Cup winners are awarded qualification into the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]], but because Arsenal qualified for the UEFA Champions League via their league position, the UEFA Cup place was passed to Southampton, the runners-up.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.premierleague.com/content/premierleague/en-gb/fans/faqs/who-qualifies-to-play-in-europe.html|title=Who qualifies to play in Europe?|work=Premier League|access-date=4 January 2013|archive-date=17 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140517152145/http://www.premierleague.com/content/premierleague/en-gb/fans/faqs/who-qualifies-to-play-in-europe.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both the [[BBC]] and [[Sky Sports]], with [[BBC One]] providing the free-to-air coverage and Sky Sports 1 being the pay-TV alternative.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup final: Arsenal v Southampton |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=17 May 2003 |page=B3 }}</ref> BBC One held the majority of the viewership, with a peak audience of 9.6 million viewers (55.7% viewing share) watching at 16:50pm and the match averaged at 8.3 million (55%) – the highest audience for a FA Cup final in four years.<ref name=ratings>{{cite news|title=Ratings – FA Cup's big gunners shoot to win on BBC1 |url=http://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/ratings-fa-cups-big-gunners-shoot-to-win-on-bbc1/1118331.article |access-date=4 January 2013 |work=[[Broadcast (magazine)|Broadcast]] |location=London |date=23 May 2003}}</ref> Coverage of the final began on the channel at 12:10pm and averaged 5.3 million (44.4%).<ref name=ratings/> The [[Match of the Day]] coverage concluded at different times dependent on station, with the broadcast in Scotland ending 10 minutes before the main broadcast finished.

